#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""queue_mvp.py — an end-to-end MVP of agents working a durable job-queue service.

WHAT THIS IS
------------
A single-file, stdlib-only *interim orchestrator*. It demonstrates the whole
money-path shape of the ops-automation design against the running job-queue service
service, with mock (planted-ground-truth) work instead of real STARS failures:

  1. seed    four mock jobs (triage / debug / code / debug-hard) into the queue
             via POST /v1/jobs, idempotently (409 -> adopt the existing id).
  2. run     for each job: claim it (lease token), build a tier-framed prompt,
             run ONE API-lane agent attempt through `claude-api-agent`, parse a
             strict JSON response contract out of the envelope's `result`, and
             apply an objective acceptance check.
                 accepted            -> POST transition done (+ result JSON)
                 needs_help/rejected -> POST transition failed+retryable, wait
                                        out the store's backoff ladder, re-claim,
                                        and escalate the MODEL TIER:
                                            attempt 1  sonnet-5  medium  junior
                                            attempt 2  opus-5    high    senior
                                            attempt 3  fable-5   high    tech lead
             The ladder is never allowed to exhaust into a tombstone: a failing
             attempt 3 creates a `human` job (parent_job_id = the original) and
             the original is transitioned *done* pointing at it.
  3. kb      one more agent pass distills every lesson_learned collected above
             (including from failed and escalated attempts) into a knowledge
             corpus written to <state-dir>/kb/corpus.md.
  4. human   a `human`-class review job is created and LEFT PENDING, so the
             human category is visible in the queue.
  5. report  handoff/queue-mvp/report.md: per-job attempt tables, the final
             result JSON, the verbatim `transitions` audit rows read straight
             from the SQLite file (there is no REST route for them), every
             lesson, and the spend section with TWO LEDGERS THAT ARE NEVER
             SUMMED (API-lane dollars vs the operator's subscription seat).

WHERE IT SITS
-------------
This script IS the orchestrator for now. A real orchestrator agent — and the
job-queue service's worker pool that will do this without a human in the loop — is future
work (see handoff/design/ops-architecture-v1.md). Everything durable lives in the
service's database plus <state-dir>/orchestrator.log, so a killed run is
resumable: seeding adopts, terminal jobs are reported instead of re-run, and the
attempt ledger is rewritten to disk after every event.

Design points worth knowing before editing:
  * attempts increment ON CLAIM ONLY (internal/store/jobs.go ClaimJob), so
    "attempt N" == job.attempts after the Nth claim, and max_attempts default 3
    is exactly the three-rung tier ladder.
  * failed+retryable with attempts >= max_attempts TOMBSTONES the job
    (internal/store/jobs.go, the poison-pill guard). This orchestrator therefore
    never sends failed+retryable on the last rung.
  * after failed+retryable the row's eligible_at is now + BackoffFor(attempts)
    (5s, 15s, 1m, 2m, 5m — internal/store/time.go), and ClaimJob's WHERE demands
    eligible_at <= now. So we poll GET /v1/jobs/{id}, sleep the delta, re-claim.
  * `deferred` REQUIRES eligible_at (timed parking) — used only by the budget
    stop, with eligible_at = now + 24h.

Usage:
    queue-mvp [all|seed|run|report] [--base-url URL] [--db PATH]
                       [--state-dir DIR] [--report PATH] [--max-api-usd 5.0]
                       [--dry-run]

--dry-run makes no `claude-api-agent` calls and spends nothing: it replays
embedded canned responses through the REAL acceptance functions, which makes it
the loop's unit test (A succeeds at tier 1; B escalates then succeeds; C
succeeds; D is confidently wrong at tier 1, rejected, then succeeds at tier 2).
"""

